#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e671c06-778b-5af4-8cc3-45acc21065c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720255} {:answer/id #uuid "3c49b7e2-cf2e-5375-86f9-369cf9b8bfbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720257} {:answer/id #uuid "58f69f77-af14-5135-b5e0-c075011d8630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 - \\cos{\\left(1 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720267} {:answer/id #uuid "74241b8a-5d22-50da-9682-145985afb0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9 \\pi^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720271} {:answer/id #uuid "f98093e0-0a2b-51c3-85d7-07abe2f34312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3 \\pi^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720282}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720255} :answer-field/id #uuid "42f4c21d-e8df-5ab5-ad92-d3ce083dd751" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "56d29d45-6d8a-5ecc-a2b5-b9af9ac06aa0" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of increasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)},\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=2 x \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 y$ and $Q=- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x y+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x+g'(y)&=- x^{2} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x \\\\ g'(y)&=0 \\\\ g(y)&=0+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3 x y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(0,0) \\\\ B&=(0,\\frac{\\pi}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\right)}- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=0 \\\\ f(A)&=\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(0\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720269 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03dedc79-34e1-59d4-8222-719f48cb1c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 e^{1 + \\pi^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720252} {:answer/id #uuid "551a62c5-f7bc-5d33-8101-6505193db506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 e^{1 + \\pi^{2}} + 6 e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720265} {:answer/id #uuid "b8046797-3880-5ecc-b164-b610aa433192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12 \\pi e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720275} {:answer/id #uuid "c40a7d4d-f866-54d3-9dac-d7dc6acd9040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 e^{1 + \\pi^{2}} + 3 e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720279} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f1058d-2db1-553a-b085-37242105597f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\pi \\left(e^{1 + \\pi^{2}} + 2 e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720279} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bea5773-7e77-56d5-bb03-57bc6666f1d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "68fe3983-c421-582b-822f-03b8b78548b5" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of decreasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t^{2} + 1,t\\rangle,\\,t\\in [\\pi,2 \\pi].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=- 3 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=3 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+g'(y)&=3 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} \\\\ g'(y)&=0 \\\\ g(y)&=0+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(1 + 4 \\pi^{2},2 \\pi) \\\\ B&=(1 + \\pi^{2},\\pi)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=- 3 e^{\\left(1 + \\pi^{2}\\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)} \\\\ &=3 e^{1 + \\pi^{2}} \\\\ f(A)&=- 3 e^{\\left(1 + 4 \\pi^{2}\\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(2 \\pi\\right) \\right)} \\\\ &=- 3 e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(3 e^{1 + \\pi^{2}}\\right)-\\left(- 3 e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =3 e^{1 + \\pi^{2}} + 3 e^{1 + 4 \\pi^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used $e^{u+v}=e^u e^v$ to factor the common exponential."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4695df43-c705-5c44-a481-41f64c38a005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720260} {:answer/id #uuid "5263914f-307d-5d5d-ab43-fa2f6c4b092e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "588" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720263} {:answer/id #uuid "60472fb1-f480-591f-93f0-05327d047c76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720268} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7a8961-53bc-5a0d-bae7-f0e9388cf105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720276} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf7041c-306b-5451-a186-d200b268ea3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{183}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720260} :answer-field/id #uuid "5205aeec-d943-5757-9534-f5aa670e3c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2fe8e07-9df6-5fc6-a30d-bb5b8941c391" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of decreasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 t,t^{2} - 2\\rangle,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 6 x y + 2 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x^{2} + 2 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=- 6 x y + 2 y$ and $Q=- 3 x^{2} + 2 x.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} y + 2 x y+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 3 x^{2} + 2 x+g'(y)&=- 3 x^{2} + 2 x \\\\ g'(y)&=0 \\\\ g(y)&=0+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 x^{2} y + 2 x y\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(6,2) \\\\ B&=(3,-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=- 3 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(-1\\right)+2 \\left(3\\right) \\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=21 \\\\ f(A)&=- 3 \\left(6\\right)^{2} \\left(2\\right)+2 \\left(6\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\\\ &=-192\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(21\\right)-\\left(-192\\right) \\\\  & =213.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009964fa-eb50-5edd-a15d-917054d2c44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-134 - 12 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} - \\frac{3 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720250} {:answer/id #uuid "11e8ff2c-5326-5029-b0e5-b3df0af661df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1330}{3} - \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{3} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720256} {:answer/id #uuid "71c7bef9-184c-5196-b187-1806754c56eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1330}{3} - 27 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{3} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{3} + 12 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720270} {:answer/id #uuid "939c4167-f78e-5cf4-bcfd-ecb19da9edea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-130 - 27 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{3} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{3} + 12 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720273} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc11438-5926-56bf-b731-225c85695fcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15 - 3 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{2} + 3 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720270} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a39c33e-fe02-58be-a763-723aaee2d0c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f3db9654-082a-56c9-81a4-7f1a4c91d60a" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C$, traversed in the direction of increasing $t$, where\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},t^{2}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 x^{2} - 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x - 2 y^{2}\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Write $P=- 3 x^{2} - 3 y$ and $Q=- 3 x - 2 y^{2}.$ A potential must satisfy $f_x=P$ and $f_y=Q$. Integrating the first component with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x^{3} - 3 x y+g(y)\n$$\n\nMatch its $y$ derivative to the second component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- 3 x+g'(y)&=- 3 x - 2 y^{2} \\\\ g'(y)&=- 2 y^{2} \\\\ g(y)&=- \\frac{2 y^{3}}{3}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoose $K=0$, since a constant cancels in the endpoint difference. Thus\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x^{3} - 3 x y - \\frac{2 y^{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating verifies both components of the given field. For the stated orientation, the starting and ending points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A&=(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)},4) \\\\ B&=(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)},9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the potential at each endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(B)&=- \\left(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\right)^{3}- \\frac{2 \\left(9\\right)^{3}}{3}- 3 \\left(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\right) \\left(9\\right) \\\\ &=-486 - 27 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{3} \\\\ f(A)&=- \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\right)^{3}- \\frac{2 \\left(4\\right)^{3}}{3}- 3 \\left(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\right) \\left(4\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{128}{3} - 12 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe fundamental theorem for line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(B)-f(A) \\\\  & =\\left(-486 - 27 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{3}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{128}{3} - 12 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{3}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{1330}{3} - 27 \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} - \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}^{3} + \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}^{3} + 12 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "533ee1c9-0188-5a43-be9f-fea528622083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720292} {:answer/id #uuid "6637bbca-84a7-54de-8ede-a3b31b57079c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720293} {:answer/id #uuid "d617fc63-f280-53bf-934a-0b24f3f24fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720300} {:answer/id #uuid "d79c9342-0e42-5a1b-9a49-a8d73ba7cdb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720301} {:answer/id #uuid "e4941109-9ceb-595a-a836-b1f3cda45c96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720293} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc5edeed-c145-5b89-87ce-ab1393f570ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "39b2ffe9-2b9d-5d3a-b611-27d9bd9454ee" :question/problem "The vertices of $\\triangle ABC$ are $A(1,0,1)$, $B(3,2,1)$, and $C(2,1,3)$. Let $H$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to the line $AB$.\n\nThe following values have been calculated from the vertices:\n\n$$\nAB=2\\sqrt{2},\\qquad \\left|\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}\\right|=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the area relationship\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle ABC}=\\frac12\\left|\\overrightarrow{AB}\\times\\overrightarrow{AC}\\right|=\\frac12 AB\\cdot CH\n$$\n\nto calculate the length of the altitude $CH$." :question/worked-solution "The cross-product magnitude is the area of the parallelogram, so the triangle has area\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle ABC}=\\frac12(4\\sqrt{2})=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe altitude $CH$ corresponds to the base $AB$. Substituting into the triangle area formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sqrt{2}&=\\frac12(2\\sqrt{2})CH\\\\\n2\\sqrt{2}&=\\sqrt{2}\\,CH\\\\\nCH&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $2$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720298 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133ef54c-a3d0-58d3-be3b-ba76efd5ddd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720288} {:answer/id #uuid "a287ad0a-439c-5eda-ad80-6d5220373ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720297} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6a4560-4533-55f3-b08f-ab5c776d28b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720299} {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb9c48-6b79-5b09-936a-349264a46dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720304} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f5501a-1e0b-5252-84cd-3fde75eb08d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720305}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720305} :answer-field/id #uuid "69aa44f5-e237-5291-814c-0003771eabca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b4631ca7-5f6d-5298-bd59-b28bd289f3eb" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $MH$ of $\\triangle LMN$, where $L(1,1,1)$, $M(3,0,2)$, and $N(2,3,3)$. Here $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $M$ to the line $LN$." :question/worked-solution "The altitude $MH$ corresponds to the base $LN$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac12 LN\\cdot MH.\n$$\n\nFirst form two edge vectors with the same starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{LN}&=\\langle 2-1,3-1,3-1\\rangle=\\langle 1,2,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\overrightarrow{LM}&=\\langle 3-1,0-1,2-1\\rangle=\\langle 2,-1,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate their cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{LN}\\times\\overrightarrow{LM}\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1&2&2\\\\2&-1&1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\\n&=(2\\cdot1-2\\cdot(-1))\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad -(1\\cdot1-2\\cdot2)\\mathbf j\\\\\n&\\quad +(1\\cdot(-1)-2\\cdot2)\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=4\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j-5\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=\\langle 4,3,-5\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts magnitude gives the parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\left|\\overrightarrow{LN}\\times\\overrightarrow{LM}\\right|=\\sqrt{4^2+3^2+(-5)^2}=\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore the triangle area and the length of the corresponding base are\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle LMN}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}2,\\qquad LN=\\sqrt{1^2+2^2+2^2}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the area formula and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}2&=\\frac12(3)MH,\\\\\n5\\sqrt{2}&=3MH,\\\\\nMH&=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{2}}3$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720306 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba2c3b7-1efe-528b-8654-b50e9e84da34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720287} {:answer/id #uuid "34ea220e-07ce-5b15-983d-791d4af82733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{29}}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720289} {:answer/id #uuid "52807fff-353c-57b7-8c20-c2d18781effa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720291} {:answer/id #uuid "75b2533d-2e79-5450-bce4-d087557bc578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720295} {:answer/id #uuid "93163197-9c37-5d31-bbad-e3e2f436f64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720291} :answer-field/id #uuid "06192684-e216-5ef8-99f7-2b283ec5fe78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f943c01c-8bad-518f-b536-c0f3f8e20ecc" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $RH$ of $\\triangle PQR$, where $P(1,-1,2)$, $Q(3,0,4)$, and $R(2,1,2)$. Here $H$ is the foot of the perpendicular from $R$ to the line $PQ$.\n\nTo help you, the cross product has been calculated:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}=\\langle -4,2,3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nIts magnitude is the area of the parallelogram spanned by the two edge vectors." :question/worked-solution "Since the altitude is drawn from $R$ to the line $PQ$, its corresponding base is $PQ$. From the vertices,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}&=\\langle 3-1,0-(-1),4-2\\rangle=\\langle 2,1,2\\rangle,\\\\\nPQ&=\\sqrt{2^2+1^2+2^2}=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe supplied cross product has magnitude\n\n$$\n\\left|\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}\\right|=\\sqrt{(-4)^2+2^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{29}.\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the area of that parallelogram:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle PQR}=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the matching base and altitude,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal A_{\\triangle PQR}&=\\frac12 PQ\\cdot RH,\\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}2&=\\frac12(3)RH,\\\\\nRH&=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe altitude is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{29}}3$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fc75ed9-0aa6-5b67-bd38-ace5ca42685d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{101}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720450} :answer-field/id #uuid "43345457-3e60-561f-8376-58b94e734f19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2469e5e1-9ace-5503-8e89-e56bcda81cfd" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -4,-4,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1,-2,5\\rangle$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product using a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\-4 & -4 & 1\\\\1 & -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=((-4)\\cdot (5)-(1)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}\\\\&\\quad-((-4)\\cdot (5)-(1)\\cdot (1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\&\\quad+((-4)\\cdot (-2)-(-4)\\cdot (1))\\mathbf{k}\\\\&=\\langle -18,21,12\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude gives the corresponding parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM&=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\|\\\\&=\\sqrt{(-18)^{2}+(21)^{2}+(12)^{2}}\\\\&=\\sqrt{909}=3\\sqrt{101}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the area of the parallelogram.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\frac12 M=\\frac{3\\sqrt{101}}{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{101}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a7f8863-e0ea-59e2-995f-70b9f1b1bc08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{357}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720448} :answer-field/id #uuid "06744f2d-f77e-5550-b7a4-02350de8ddea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3bd1c273-9de7-5fec-8d76-8c17e57567cb" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -2,-3,2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -1,-2,-4\\rangle$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Compute the cross product using a determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}&\\mathbf{j}&\\mathbf{k}\\\\-2 & -3 & 2\\\\-1 & -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=((-3)\\cdot (-4)-(2)\\cdot (-2))\\mathbf{i}\\\\&\\quad-((-2)\\cdot (-4)-(2)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}\\\\&\\quad+((-2)\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k}\\\\&=\\langle 16,-10,1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe magnitude gives the corresponding parallelogram area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM&=\\|\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\|\\\\&=\\sqrt{(16)^{2}+(-10)^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\\\&=\\sqrt{357}=\\sqrt{357}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle has half the area of the parallelogram.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA&=\\frac12 M=\\frac{\\sqrt{357}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{357}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186720446 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662e87b7-f526-5570-98c9-2553d43d8132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175